    "problem_statement": "[Bug]: File overwrite behavior in PySnooper is reversed\n\n### Bug summary\n\nI've discovered an issue with PySnooper's file writing functionality. When using PySnooper to output to a file, the overwrite behavior seems to be reversed from what would be expected.\n\n### Code for reproduction\n\n```python\nimport pysnooper\n\n# First function with default behavior (should append)\n@pysnooper.snoop('debug_output.log')\ndef function1():\n    x = 1\n    y = 2\n    return x + y\n\n# Second function with explicit overwrite=True (should overwrite)\n@pysnooper.snoop('debug_output.log', overwrite=True)\ndef function2():\n    a = 3\n    b = 4\n    return a + b\n\n# Run both functions\nfunction1()\nfunction2()\n```\n\n### Actual outcome\n\nWhen running the code above:\n1. The first function call with default settings (no explicit overwrite parameter) overwrites any existing content in the file instead of appending to it.\n2. The second function call with `overwrite=True` appends to the file instead of overwriting it.\n\nThis is the opposite of the expected behavior based on the parameter name and documentation.\n\n### Expected outcome\n\nI would expect:\n1. By default (no overwrite parameter specified), PySnooper should append to the existing log file.\n2. When `overwrite=True` is specified, PySnooper should clear the file before writing new content.\n\n### Additional information\n\nThis issue makes it difficult to use PySnooper for debugging multiple functions in sequence, as the default behavior unexpectedly overwrites previous debug information instead of preserving it.\n\n### Operating system\n\nUbuntu 20.04\n\n### Python version\n\nPython 3.8.10\n\n### Installation\n\npip install pysnooper==0.4.3"
